---
components: ["StateTimeline"]
description: State timeline shows what state each of several things was in over a shared stretch of time, one row each.
---

<script>
  import { StateTimeline } from "carbon-components-svelte/viz";

  const at = (hour, minute = 0) => new Date(2026, 8, 21, hour, minute);
  const health = [
    { service: "api", status: "ok", from: at(0), to: at(9, 40) },
    { service: "api", status: "degraded", from: at(9, 40), to: at(10, 15) },
    { service: "api", status: "ok", from: at(10, 15), to: at(24) },
    { service: "checkout", status: "ok", from: at(0), to: at(14) },
    { service: "checkout", status: "down", from: at(14), to: at(14, 25) },
    { service: "checkout", status: "degraded", from: at(14, 25), to: at(15, 30) },
    { service: "checkout", status: "ok", from: at(15, 30), to: at(24) },
    { service: "worker", status: "ok", from: at(0), to: at(3) },
    { service: "worker", status: "down", from: at(3), to: at(5, 30) },
    { service: "worker", status: "ok", from: at(5, 30), to: at(24) },
    { service: "search", status: "ok", from: at(0), to: at(24) },
  ];
  const machine = ["Idle", "Running", "Idle", "Running", "Maintenance", "Running", "Idle"].map((state, i) => ({
    line: "Line 3",
    state,
    from: at(i * 3),
    to: at(i * 3 + 3),
  }));

  let selected = null;
</script>

`StateTimeline` is part of the data visualization subpackage. Import it from `carbon-components-svelte/viz` and load `carbon-components-svelte/css/viz.css` alongside `all.css`. With a single-theme stylesheet such as `g100.css`, load the matching `viz-g100.css` instead. The subpackage is experimental: its API may change in a minor release.

For one thing's history by fixed period, such as a day at a time, [UptimeBar](/components/UptimeBar) is simpler.

## Basic

Pass one row for each span: a `row` it belongs to, its `state`, and when it `start`s and `end`s, as a `Date`, a timestamp, or a date string. Rows keep first-seen order, spans within a row are sorted by start, and every row shares one time scale from the earliest start to the latest end. Hover a span to read its state, its times, and how long it lasted.

<StateTimeline data={health} row="service" state="status" start="from" end="to" title="Service health, today" />

## State colors

States take the categorical colors in first-seen order. Set `states` to give each a color: a semantic name such as `"success"`, a categorical index, a viz token name, or any CSS color.

<StateTimeline
  data={health}
  row="service"
  state="status"
  start="from"
  end="to"
  states={{ ok: "success", degraded: "warning", down: "error" }}
  title="Service health, today"
/>

## Domain

Set `domain` to show a fixed range. Spans are clipped to it, and a span entirely outside is dropped.

<StateTimeline
  data={health}
  row="service"
  state="status"
  start="from"
  end="to"
  states={{ ok: "success", degraded: "warning", down: "error" }}
  domain={[at(8), at(16)]}
  title="Service health, working hours"
/>

## Sizes

<StateTimeline data={machine} row="line" state="state" start="from" end="to" size="lg" title="Line 3, today" />

## Selectable

Set `selectable` to turn spans into toggle buttons. Bind `selected`, a span's id, or listen for `select`, whose detail carries the span and its `datum`.

<StateTimeline
  data={health}
  row="service"
  state="status"
  start="from"
  end="to"
  states={{ ok: "success", degraded: "warning", down: "error" }}
  title="Pick an incident"
  selectable
  bind:selected
/>

## Accessibility

The timeline is a table: the `title` is its caption and each row's name is a row header. Every span holds its state, its start and end, and its length as text, such as "down, Sep 21, 2026, 2:00 PM to Sep 21, 2026, 2:25 PM, 25m", so nothing rests on color. Set `rowHeader` to name the first column, `locale` for the times, and `utc` to place the axis ticks on UTC boundaries. When `selectable`, the spans share one tab stop and the arrow keys move between them.
